# Line endings are the repository's, not the checkout's.
#
# Git on Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, so every text file arrives with CRLF
# unless something says otherwise. Nothing did, and the first CI run on Windows lost seven
# tests in `tests/tour.test.ts` at once: the walker reads HTML fixtures and writes an
# accessibility snapshot, and a snapshot compared against a string in the test cannot
# survive its bytes changing on checkout. A heading came back `undefined`, a list of
# section anchors came back empty, and a state hash that had to differ did not.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises what enters the repository and checks it out with LF
# everywhere, so a fixture is the same file on the three systems the tests run on.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Left exactly as they are: a byte changed in any of these is a corrupted file, not a
# line ending. Git's own detection usually gets this right; naming them means it cannot
# get it wrong.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.svg text eol=lf
*.webm binary
*.mp4 binary
*.mp3 binary
*.wav binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
